    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-39135",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882",
      "knowledge_point": "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient",
      "problem": "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+7x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8 & 7 & 3 \\\\  &  & -6 & -6 & -3 \\\\  & 2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+3}=2{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+3)(2{x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)^{3}+8(0)^{2}+7(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png)",
