    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-262221",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions",
      "problem": "The distance between the points $A(3,1,2)$ and $B(5,-3,4)$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,1,2)}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,\u00a0}{y}_{1}\\text{,\u00a0}{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(5,-3,4)}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,\u00a0}{y}_{2}\\text{,\u00a0}{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
