      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Function",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}$ is defined for $x>-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find ${f}^{-1}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{(2y+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{(2y+1)^{2}} \\\\ (2y+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2y+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\ |2y+1| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 2y+1 & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>-\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n|2y+1|=2y+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
